Finding the right CRM can be the difference between a chaotic lead pile and a predictable pipeline. We compared the real estate CRMs solo agents, teams, and brokerages actually use in 2026 — evaluating lead routing, automation, IDX/website options, and total cost of ownership — then segmented the results by who each tool is genuinely best for.

The real estate CRM market has matured significantly — most tools now offer some form of AI-assisted follow-up and SMS automation. The differentiators are in the details: how cleanly leads flow in from Zillow and Realtor.com, how easy it is to hand off a lead to a showing agent, and how well each platform fits the way you actually work — solo, team, or brokerage.

## A note on LionDesk (and why most 2026 lists are wrong)

If you searched for a real estate CRM in the last few years, you probably saw LionDesk near the top of every list. It no longer exists. Lone Wolf Technologies shut it down in September 2025 and moved its 165,000+ agents to a new platform called Lone Wolf Relationships. Many "best real estate CRM 2026" articles still list LionDesk because they were copied from older roundups and never rechecked — a useful reminder to confirm any recommendation against the vendor's own site before you buy.

## CRM you own vs. CRM your brokerage gives you

Brokerage-provided platforms (BoldTrail, BoomTown, Sierra Interactive) are often excellent, but you typically lose access to your data the day you change brokerages. If you might switch, a portable CRM you pay for yourself (Follow Up Boss, Wise Agent) keeps your contacts and history with you.
